One page of an events list response, in ascending seq order.

The five events in "data" are exactly the five single-event fixtures in this directory, unchanged:
event-added-document.json, event-reordered-section.json, event-updated-numbering-standard.json,
event-deleted-standard.json, and event-nondeliverable-standard.json. Their provenance, and which parts
are verbatim gate G-4 captures, is documented in each of those .source files. This envelope exists so
that feed-level tests have a real page to walk, while converter-level tests can read a single resource
object without an envelope in the way.

Envelope structure:
  https://developerdocs.instructure.com/services/ab-connect/introduction/paging.md
  https://developerdocs.instructure.com/services/ab-connect/reference/events.md
  01-sdk-v3.md sections 5.5 and 6.1.

links.self shows the request the version 3 builder emits for a delta read: filter[events]=(seq GT n),
sort[events]=seq, and all eleven EventFieldSet.Full field names. AB Connect's default ordering is by
relevance rather than sequence, so the sort must be requested every time (section 5.4 rule 7). The
query carries no credentials; a fixture must never contain a partner id or a signature.

Deliberate properties of this fixture:
  - The sequence numbers are 702955, 703118, 703204, 703311, 703486: ascending but NOT contiguous.
    AB Connect documents this: "Sequence numbers will not appear incremental ... Events received by any
    individual partner will not have incremental numbers". Section 6.1 requires that the SDK perform no
    gap detection and log nothing about gaps, so a test that reads this page must not treat the gaps as
    an error.
  - links.next is null and meta.count is 5, which is the last page of a walk. Section 6.1's traversal
    re-anchors on the highest seq seen (703486) rather than following links.next at all.
  - One of the five is a deliverability event and four are standard-level changes, so a routing test
    can partition the page by ABEvent.IsDeliveryEvent and get 1 and 4.
